Given an n × m binary matrix grid[][], find the total count of all cells containing 1 that are unable to move out of the grid through a path of adjacent 1s.
Adjacency means you can only move in four directions: Up, Down, Left, and Right. Diagonal moves are not allowed.
Assume that the space immediately outside the grid is an open path. Any 1 located directly on the outer boundary of the grid (first row, last row, first column, or last column) can immediately step out, and any 1 connected to it can follow and also step out of the grid.
// Solution for the problem statement 
class Solution {
    int cntOnes(int[][] grid) {
        int n = grid.length;
        if (n == 0) return 0;
        int m = grid[0].length;

        // Step 1: Traverse the left and right boundaries
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            if (grid[i][0] == 1) dfs(grid, i, 0, n, m);
            if (grid[i][m - 1] == 1) dfs(grid, i, m - 1, n, m);
        }

        // Step 2: Traverse the top and bottom boundaries
        for (int j = 0; j < m; j++) {
            if (grid[0][j] == 1) dfs(grid, 0, j, n, m);
            if (grid[n - 1][j] == 1) dfs(grid, n - 1, j, n, m);
        }

        // Step 3: Count the remaining 1s that are trapped
        int trappedCount = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            for (int j = 0; j < m; j++) {
                if (grid[i][j] == 1) {
                    trappedCount++;
                }
            }
        }

        return trappedCount;
    }

    private void dfs(int[][] grid, int r, int c, int n, int m) {
        // Base case: check bounds and if cell is a 1
        if (r < 0 || r >= n || c < 0 || c >= m || grid[r][c] != 1) {
            return;
        }

        // Change 1 to 0 to mark it as escaped/visited
        grid[r][c] = 0;

        // Recursively visit all 4 adjacent neighbors
        dfs(grid, r + 1, c, n, m);
        dfs(grid, r - 1, c, n, m);
        dfs(grid, r, c + 1, n, m);
        dfs(grid, r, c - 1, n, m);
    }
}
